## Justification

• The function splits the sentence into individual words.
• Then it utilizes the set data structure to automatically remove duplicate words.
• Finally, the length of the set is returned, which effectively gives the count of unique words in the sentence.

## Explanation of Justified Choices

Choosing to use a set was the instinctive approach because sets naturally handle duplicates by ignoring them. In contrast, lists would maintain duplicate items, requiring an additional step to remove them before counting.

## Extra Requirements

• The function supports sentences with a length as specified, from 1 to 1000 words. As suggested by the number of test cases and the nature of set operations, this implementation would handle sentences of those lengths without difficulty.

## Refinement Possibilities

• If the function was needed to handle more than distinct words (for example, if two distinct sentences had to be compared), a dictionary could be used where each word is the key, and its count is the value. This would provide additional info for word frequency, but would still only require a single iteration over the split sentences.
